    {
      "id": "LBMM",
      "code_hash": "dcc26fb14a9b2c2910257ea41698b6b4",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"post-training-quantization-ptq\">Post Training Quantization (PTQ)</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The basic flow is as follow:</span>\n<ol>\n<li>Export the model to to a stable, backend-agnostic format that\u2019s suitable for transformations, optimizations, and deployment.</li>\n<li>Define the quantizer that will prepare the model for quantization. Here I used the X86 for CPU deployments, but there is a simple variant that works better for mobile and edge devices working on ARM CPUs.</li>\n<li>Preparing the model for quantization. For example, folding batch-norm into preceding conv2d operators, and inserting observers in appropriate places to collect activation statistics needed for calibration.</li>\n<li>Running inference on calibration data to collect activation statistics</li>\n<li>Converts calibrated model to a quantized model. While the quantized model already takes less space, it is not yet optimized for the final deployment.</li>\n</ol></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
